<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Upper Swabia</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> is the region of south-western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> between the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iller">Iller</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>, in modern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/holy-roman-empire">Holy Roman Empire</a> it was divided among many small territories, including imperial abbeys such as Weingarten, imperial cities such as Biberach and Ravensburg, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> lands. In 1525 its peasants drew up the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/twelve-articles">Twelve Articles</a>, the programme of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants' War</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/upper-swabia.jpg" alt="Upper Swabia" width="320" height="201" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Maximilian Dörrbecker (Chumwa) · CC BY-SA 2.5 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_Upper_Swabia_(Oberschwaben).png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Upper <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> lies on the Iller-Lech Plateau, also called the Upper Swabian Plain, and was shaped by glaciers retreating after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/riss">Riss</a> glaciation, which left many hollows that filled with water and gave the region its many lakes. The hilly land rises from about 458 metres in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> valley to 833 metres in the south-west and falls to 395 metres at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lake-constance">Lake Constance</a>. The European watershed crosses it, so that some rivers drain to the Danube and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a> and others to Lake Constance and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/north-sea">North Sea</a>. Its eastern border has long been disputed: historically the Lech divided Swabia from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Finds around the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/federsee">Federsee</a> near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bad-buchau">Bad Buchau</a> show settlement since the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/neolithic">Neolithic</a>. Until about 260 CE the area belonged to the Roman province of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/raetia">Raetia</a>, after which the Alamanni settled it. In the Merovingian period it came under the Frankish kings and the Christianization of the region began, and later it was part of the Duchy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabia">Swabia</a> before breaking up, from the High Middle Ages, into many small units.</p><p>The region was a centre of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants' War</a>, with three bands, the Allgäuer Haufen, the Seehaufen and the Baltringer Haufen, and many castles and monasteries were destroyed. By July 1525 the rising had been crushed, with countless peasants killed in battle or punished afterwards, and the legal position of the peasants did not change for 300 years. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thirty-years-war">Thirty Years War</a> armies and plague left the region severely depopulated.</p><p>After the war the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/counter-reformation">Counter-Reformation</a> and new wealth led lords and abbeys to rebuild in the style now called Upper Swabian Baroque. In 1803 almost all monasteries were dissolved and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/free-imperial-city">Free Imperial Cities</a> lost their independence, and Catholic Upper Swabia passed to the Protestant Kingdom of Württemberg, an annexation confirmed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> in 1815.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Upper Swabia lies in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baden-wurttemberg">Baden-Württemberg</a>, formed in 1952, and in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bavaria">Bavaria</a>, and remains mainly rural, with dairy farming and hops and fruit in the Schussen basin. Its main towns include Ravensburg, Weingarten, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/biberach-an-der-riss">Biberach an der Riß</a>, Friedrichshafen and Memmingen. The Upper Swabian Baroque Route links its churches, abbeys and palaces.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Upper Swabia” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The new kingdom joined the largely Lutheran heartland on the Neckar to Catholic <b>Upper Swabia</b>, the former imperial cities and the Hohenlohe plain, creating the territory that Württemberg kept until 1918.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-wurttemberg">Kingdom of Württemberg · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>Biberach grew as a market town on the Riss under the Hohenstaufen, at the crossing of roads in <b>Upper Swabia</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/free-imperial-city-of-biberach">Free Imperial City of Biberach</a></li><li><blockquote>The abbots ruled villages and farmland in <b>Upper Swabia</b>, sat among the Swabian imperial prelates and were among the richest in the region.</blockquote><a href="/state/imperial-abbey-of-weingarten">Imperial Abbey of Weingarten</a></li></ul></section>
